<section class="section" id="masking">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Masking</p>
      <h2>23 strategies, each labelled by what it promises</h2>
      <p>Every strategy is tagged as anonymisation, pseudonymisation, synthetic replacement, or passthrough. Keyed strategies are deterministic: the same secret and the same input always produce the same output.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="strategy-grid">
      <div class="strategy">hash<span>keyed digest</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">fpe<span>FF1 format-preserving</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">faker<span>realistic replacement</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">redact<span>fixed placeholder</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">categorical<span>remap to a set</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">shuffle<span>break row identity</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">truncate<span>keep N characters</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">bucketize<span>fixed-width bins</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">top_code<span>collapse the tail</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">date_shift<span>bounded offset</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">bucket_perturb<span>snap within period</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">windowed_date<span>random in window</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">geo_generalize<span>ZIP cascade, H3</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">code_set<span>ICD-10, NDC, HCPCS</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">text_redact<span>spans to tokens</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">text_mask<span>spans to synthetic</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">joint_mask<span>columns together</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">group_key<span>shared group id</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">grouped_series<span>series per group</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">derived<span>from another column</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">derived_aggregate<span>from a group</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">formula<span>expression</span></div>
      <div class="strategy">passthrough<span>unchanged</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted" style="margin-top: var(--space-4)"><a class="link-arrow" href="/docs/reference/strategies">Strategy reference {% icon "arrow-right" %}</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="generation">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="grid grid-2">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Generation</p>
        <h2>Synthetic rows from a spec, or from the shape of real data</h2>
        <p class="muted">Twelve generation types and 34 domain providers (names, addresses, SSN, NPI, MRN, NDC, IBAN, PAN, CUSIP, ICD-10 and more). Run <code>decoy fit</code> on a real table to capture histograms, quantiles, and top-k values, then sample from that snapshot instead of inventing a distribution.</p>
        <p class="muted">Marginal differential privacy is available for generated data: per-column mechanisms from OpenDP with a single (epsilon, delta) budget. It covers the column types you declare and does not extend to relationships between columns or to masked output. <a href="/docs/concepts/differential-privacy">What it does and does not cover</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <ul class="stack" style="list-style: none; padding: 0; color: var(--fg-muted); font-size: var(--text-sm)">
          <li><strong class="tag-safe">categorical, distribution, sequence</strong> for shape</li>
          <li><strong class="tag-safe">faker, formula, derived, derived_aggregate</strong> for values</li>
          <li><strong class="tag-safe">reference, group_key, grouped_series</strong> for child tables that point at generated parents</li>
          <li><strong class="tag-safe">statistical, windowed_date</strong> for fitted snapshots and time windows</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="subsetting">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="grid grid-2">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Subsetting</p>
        <h3>A small, referentially intact slice</h3>
        <p>Start from a filter on one table and walk the declared relationships to a fixpoint under a fan-out budget. Composite and self-referencing keys are supported; nulls pass through; orphan rows follow the policy you set: preserve, remap, warn, or fail. Parquet in, Parquet out.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card" id="detection">
        <p class="eyebrow">Detection</p>
        <h3>STORM finds it, you decide</h3>
        <p>STORM profiles every column with 25 value detectors and tags each finding with a domain: identity, financial, health, contact, location. After a run, <code>decoy storm integrity</code> checks the output for residual PII and FK parity and returns exit code 4 on findings. An optional spaCy model adds person and location detection in free text.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="disguises">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Disguises</p>
      <h2>Policy templates for the frameworks you answer to</h2>
      <p>A Disguise is a dated, versioned bundle of column-pattern-to-strategy rules. Ten ship with the engine: HIPAA Safe Harbor (21 rules), PCI, GDPR, GLBA, CCPA, CPNI, FERPA, SOX, property and casualty insurance, and a default. They are templates that encode a reading of each framework, not certifications.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cluster">
      <span class="pill pill--accent">hipaa</span><span class="pill pill--accent">pci</span><span class="pill pill--accent">gdpr</span><span class="pill pill--accent">glba</span><span class="pill pill--accent">ccpa</span><span class="pill pill--accent">cpni</span><span class="pill pill--accent">ferpa</span><span class="pill pill--accent">sox</span><span class="pill pill--accent">pc</span><span class="pill pill--accent">default</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="determinism">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="grid grid-2">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Determinism and joins</p>
        <h2>Same secret, same input, same output. Everywhere.</h2>
        <p class="muted">Keyed strategies derive their output with HKDF-SHA256 from a mask secret you supply (at least 32 bytes, from an environment variable or a file) and the source value. Tables that share a namespace mask the same value the same way, so joins between them survive and output is byte-stable across runs.</p>
        <p class="muted">Format-preserving encryption uses NIST SP 800-38G FF1 with AES-256 and refuses domains under one million values. FPE columns reverse from the config alone; one-way columns can be made reversible by writing an encrypted token vault.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="terminal">
        <div class="terminal-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>pipeline.yaml</span></div>
<pre>version: 1
global_settings:
  mask_secret_ref: env:DECOY_MASK_SECRET
sources:
  - name: customers
    type: file
    path: customers.parquet
  - name: orders
    type: file
    path: orders.parquet
tables:
  customers:
    columns:
      id:    { strategy: hash, namespace: customer_id }
      email: { strategy: faker, provider: email, deterministic: true }
      ssn:   { strategy: fpe, checksum: none }
      dob:   { strategy: date_shift, max_days: 30 }
  orders:
    columns:
      customer_id: { strategy: hash, namespace: customer_id }
relationships:
  - child: orders.customer_id
    parent: customers.id
    orphan_policy: fail</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="ci">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">CI</p>
      <h2>Made to run unattended</h2>
      <p><code>--json</code> writes results to stdout and progress to stderr. <code>--quiet</code> leaves only the exit code. <code>--evidence-out</code> records what ran without recording a single raw value, and <code>decoy evidence verify</code> checks it later.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Exit code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Typical cause</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>OK</td><td>Run completed, checks passed</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>Usage</td><td>Bad flags or arguments</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>3</code></td><td>Runtime</td><td>Unreadable input, strategy error, I/O failure</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>4</code></td><td>Findings</td><td>Residual PII, FK parity or policy violations from <code>storm integrity</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>5</code></td><td>Capacity</td><td><code>preflight</code> estimates the job cannot fit on this host</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="performance">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Performance</p>
      <h2>Measured, with the conditions attached</h2>
      <p>Masking runs on pandas with a compiled Rust kernel for the hot strategies; Arrow carries data between stages; DuckDB handles out-of-core joins for large foreign-key work. The figures below are single-host developer runs, not a marketing benchmark.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Workload</th><th>Result</th><th>Conditions</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Hash strategy, per column</td><td>About 1.04M rows/s</td><td>Native kernel, x86-64 Linux, versus about 235k rows/s on pandas</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1M rows, hash + redact + truncate + passthrough</td><td>1.27 s</td><td>4 threads; 10.7 s on pandas; byte-identical output</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100M rows, hash strategies</td><td>About 430 s, 447 MB peak</td><td>8 threads, one host</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mixed generate + mask, 10M rows</td><td>About 176k rows/s, 2.0 GB peak</td><td>4 vCPU, 12 GiB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pure generation, 10M rows</td><td>About 14k rows/s, 226 MB peak</td><td>Python path; native generation is in progress</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted" style="margin-top: var(--space-4)">Numbers are reproduced from the engine's published capacity records. Windows, macOS, and ARM64 are supported but have no published performance evidence yet.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="formats">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="grid grid-2">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Formats and limits</p>
        <h2>Files first</h2>
        <p class="muted">The CLI reads and writes CSV, Parquet, and fixed-width files on local disk, and masks folders of related tables in one run. Chunked streaming handles files larger than memory for CSV and Parquet with value-keyed strategies.</p>
        <p class="muted">Object storage and databases are a Platform feature today; the CLI tells you so at validation rather than failing mid-run. Databases round-trip through an export. <a href="/docs/reference/limits">Full list of limits</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note note--raw">
        <strong>What we do not claim.</strong> Masked output carries no differential-privacy guarantee. Free-text detection is best effort. Disguises are templates, not certifications. The engine's own <a href="/docs/reference/capabilities">feature support and limits</a> page is the source of truth.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

